  "Attributes": [
    {
      "Id": 758259,
      "attributeType": "LongString",
      "attributeName": "AIDescription",
      "attributeRelationType": "None",
      "Value": "### Bandai WonderSwan\n\nThe WonderSwan, released in Japan in 1999 by Bandai, was a unique handheld console conceived by Gunpei Yokoi of Game Boy fame, marking his final hardware project. Designed to compete with the Game Boy Color and Neo Geo Pocket Color, it distinguished itself with its portrait and landscape orientation thanks to a dual-button design. While initially monochrome, the later WonderSwan Color added a passive matrix color screen while maintaining backward compatibility. \n\nPowered by a 16-bit NEC V30 MZ processor, the WonderSwan offered a surprisingly long battery life and a library of games, including remakes of classic Squaresoft titles like *Final Fantasy* and *Final Fantasy IV*, alongside original titles from franchises like *Digimon* and *Gundam*. Despite a peak of 8% market share, the WonderSwan ultimately faded with the arrival of the Game Boy Advance, leaving behind a legacy of innovation and a dedicated cult following."
    },
